            <ArticleTitle>Diseases in the tropics: a confrontation in an African rural practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>331-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recent claims that &quot;diseases of Western civilization&quot; are rare in rural African communities may not be justified. African peasants have a high pain threshold; many attend traditional healers rather than Western-trained doctors; and their language contains no words to express the descriptive terms used in Western medicine for the symptoms and clinical features of these diseases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
